# Kiro Workflow — Repository Entry Point

This repository follows the Kiro Workflow Development Specification.
Specs, steering, skills, and custom sub-agents are managed exclusively by
the `kspec` CLI.

## If you are an AI agent (Claude Code, Cursor, Codex, Kiro IDE…)

**Read [`kspec.md`](./kspec.md) before producing any artifact.** It is the
operational guide that tells you how to drive `kspec` without reading source.
Everything below this section is a quick map; `kspec.md` is the contract.

## Layout

- `.kiro/steering/` — project memory loaded into every agent interaction.
- `.kiro/specs/<feature-name>/` — per-feature contracts (`spec.json` + `requirements.md` + `design.md` + `tasks.md`).
- `.kiro/settings/rules/` — generation rules and review gates the agent runs mechanically.
- `.kiro/settings/templates/` — templates the agent uses when generating artifacts.
- `.kiro/settings/mcp.json` — workspace-scope MCP servers.
- `.agents/skills/<skill-name>/` — executable workflow skills (`SKILL.md` + `references/` + `assets/` + `scripts/`).
- `.agents/agents/<name>.md` — custom sub-agents with least-privilege tool scope.
- `.kiroignore` — paths and patterns the agent MUST NOT read.

## Workflow at a glance

1. `kspec spec init <feature>` then iterate requirements → design → tasks.
2. Each phase requires explicit human approval recorded in `spec.json`.
3. `ready_for_implementation` flips to `true` only after all three phases are approved.
4. Implementation runs one task per iteration with fresh-context sub-agents (implementer, reviewer, debugger).
5. Steering is updated only when a new pattern emerges that the agent could not derive from the code.

## Non-negotiables

- EARS phrasing for every requirement.
- `_Requirements:_`, `_Boundary:_`, and `_Depends:_` annotations on tasks.
- `.kiroignore` configured before exposing the repo to any agent.
- Least privilege on MCP, trusted commands, and CI agents.
- Skills built from real expertise — never from generic LLM output.

